{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_014DVXJyBEXoNw3P85Po1BoP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P2kj1rSZA3BwYndEXGhw5V","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/home/firefly/code/nus/math/brownian-motion/blueprint/src/chapters/doob_meyer.tex","old_string":"\\begin{proof}\nThe proof has four separate tasks.\n\nFirst, remove predictable jumps at the fixed deterministic time \\(t\\).  Strong\npredictability gives measurability of the time section with respect to the\npredictable past sigma-algebra, not arbitrary \\(\\mathcal F_s\\)-measurability.\nUse the left-limit package\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.stronglyMeasurable_leftLim_past_of_left_approach}\nto make \\(M_{t-}\\) measurable with respect to \\(\\bigvee_{s<t}\\mathcal F_s\\)\nwhenever a deterministic cofinal left-approaching sequence is available.\nTogether with\nLemma~\\ref{lem:IsStronglyPredictable.stronglyMeasurable_past}, this gives\npast measurability of the jump \\(M_t-M_{t-}\\) through\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.stronglyMeasurable_jump_leftLim_past_of_left_approach}.\nThe next conditional-expectation step must show this past-measurable jump has\nconditional expectation zero; do not replace past-sigma-algebra measurability\nby arbitrary fixed-time \\(\\mathcal F_s\\)-measurability without proof.  The\ngenerated-past uniqueness lemma\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Filtration.ae_eq_zero_of_past_setIntegral_eq_zero} reduces\nthis to proving zero integrals of the jump on every earlier-filtration set,\nand\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_of_left_approach_past_setIntegral_zero}\npackages that reduction for \\(M_t-M_{t-}\\).  The\nset-integral identity\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.setIntegral_increment_eq_zero_of_measurableSet}\nshows that every finite increment \\(M_v-M_u\\) has zero integral over sets in\nan earlier sigma-algebra.  The dominated left-limit wrapper\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.setIntegral_jump_leftLim_eq_zero_of_left_approach_of_dominated}\nis the analytic bridge from those finite-increment identities to the jump\nintegral, once a left-approaching sequence is eventually after the fixed\nstrict-past time and an integrable dominator is available on the restricted\nset.\nIn the bounded horizon case,\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_of_left_approach_past_setIntegral_zero_of_bound}\npackages these two inputs: left convergence gives the eventual ordering for\neach strict-past time, and the deterministic horizon bound supplies the\nconstant restricted dominator.  The remaining explicit jump-integrability\nhypothesis of that lemma is supplied by\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.integrable_jump_leftLim_of_left_approach_of_bound},\nwhich bounds the jump as the limit of the bounded increments\n\\(M_t-M_{u_n}\\).  Consequently\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_of_left_approach_past_setIntegral_zero_of_bound_no_integrability}\nis the bounded-horizon predictable-jump removal statement that only assumes\nthe explicit left-approaching sequence and the deterministic a.e. horizon\nbound.\nThe stopped/localized wrappers\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_stoppedProcess_indicator_of_left_approach_of_bound}\nand\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_localizingSequence_stoppedProcess_indicator_of_left_approach_of_bound}\napply this bounded jump-removal theorem to stopped/indicator processes.\nThe stopped/original comparison is handled separately: first\nLemma~\\ref{lem:stoppedProcess_indicator_eventuallyEq_left_of_lt} identifies\nthe stopped path with the original path in a left-neighborhood of \\(t\\) on\nthe event \\(\\{t<\\tau\\}\\), then\nLemma~\\ref{lem:leftLim_stoppedProcess_indicator_eq_of_lt} transfers the\nleft limit.  Consequently\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_on_event_of_left_approach_of_bound}\nturns stopped jump-removal into original jump-removal on \\(\\{t<\\tau\\}\\), and\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_localizingSequence_of_left_approach_of_bound}\nuses the localizing-sequence cover to remove the event.\nThe older sequence lemma\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.ae_eq_leftLim_of_left_approach_past_measurable_increments}\nremains available for the stronger case where every increment is already\n\\(\\mathcal F_{u_n}\\)-measurable.\n\nSecond, localize on the deterministic horizon \\([\\bot,t]\\).  Stopping at\nbounded levels gives true martingales whose paths are bounded on the horizon,\nremain càdlàg and strongly predictable, and preserve locally bounded\nvariation.  On the event where the localizing time exceeds \\(t\\), the stopped\nprocess agrees with the original process at time \\(t\\).\n\nThird, prove the bounded continuous square-integral premise.  For the bounded\ncontinuous stopped process, apply\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.integral_sq_terminal_eq_zero_of_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation}\nto obtain integrability of \\(M_t^2\\) and the identity\n\\(\\int M_t^2\\,dP=0\\).\n\nFinally, package these bounded stopped processes with\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_stoppedProcess_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_of_variation_bound_continuousOn}.\nThis stopped-process lemma is itself a specialization of\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_localized_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_of_variation_bound_continuousOn}:\non each event where the stopping time exceeds \\(t\\), the stopped process\nagrees with the original process at \\(t\\), and the countable localization\nevents cover almost every sample point.  Equivalently, after deriving the\nsquare-integral identity directly, use\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_core}\nfor each bounded stopped process and run the same localized-family exhaustion\nargument.\n\\end{proof}","new_string":"% PIVOT (iter-062): the route stopped adding general-`κ` branch/pre-stop helpers.\n% A strategy-critic + mathlib-analogist reassessment found that this `_reduction`\n% is NOT provable over an arbitrary linearly ordered `κ`: closing it requires a\n% deterministic refining partition of `[⊥,t]` with vanishing mesh, which does not\n% exist in a general order. Every downstream endpoint already takes the partition\n% and mesh as EXPLICIT hypotheses precisely to defer this construction; `_reduction`\n% is where the buck stops. The fix is to SPECIALIZE to densely-ordered time\n% (`[DenselyOrdered κ]` with the topological instances the endpoints already carry,\n% e.g. `ℝ≥0`, where dyadic partitions + Heine–Cantor supply the mesh) and ASSEMBLE\n% the already-closed pieces — not to prove more helpers. The jump-removal and\n% continuous-localizing endpoints below are all closed; the only remaining content\n% is the partition construction and wiring.\n\\begin{proof}\nSpecialize to densely-ordered time so that a deterministic refining partition of\nthe compact horizon \\([\\bot,t]\\) with vanishing mesh can be constructed (on\n\\(\\mathbb R_{\\ge 0}\\) take dyadic subdivisions; uniform continuity of the paths on\nthe compact interval gives the vanishing-mesh control by Heine–Cantor).  With that\npartition and the deterministic a.e.\\ horizon and variation bounds available, the\nproof is pure assembly of closed lemmas; no new branch, pre-stop, or mesh helper is\nneeded.\n\n\\emph{Step 1 — bounds and partition.}  From the càdlàg paths on the compact\nhorizon \\([\\bot,t]\\) obtain an a.e.\\ deterministic horizon bound \\(\\sup_{s\\in[\\bot,t]}\\|M_s\\|\\le C\\),\nand from locally bounded variation obtain an a.e.\\ deterministic variation bound\n\\((\\operatorname{Var}_{[\\bot,t]} M)\\le V\\).  Construct the deterministic refining\npartition \\(u_n\\) of \\([\\bot,t]\\) and verify the vanishing-mesh hypothesis.\n\n\\emph{Step 2 — jump removal gives continuity on the localized pieces.}  The\npredictable-jump removal pipeline is already closed and supplies, along a localizing\nsequence, that the stopped/indicator pieces have no predictable jump, i.e.\\ are\na.e.\\ continuous on the horizon.  This is exactly the input the continuous-localizing\nendpoint consumes; the existing jump-removal wrappers (terminal-and-left-limit zero\npackages) feed it.\n\n\\emph{Step 3 — apply the closed continuous dense-left endpoint.}  Feed the bounds,\nvariation bound, partition, mesh, and continuity into\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_localizingSequence_of_bound_variation_bound_original_continuousOn_of_dense_left}\n(or, with a single localization level, its fixed-level dense-left companion\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_bound_variation_bound_original_continuousOn_of_dense_left}).\nUnder \\([\\text{DenselyOrdered }\\kappa]\\) the branch disjunction these endpoints\ninternally require is always the nontrivial strict-left branch, so the\nbottom-immediate and left-isolated cases — and the entire `leastGT` pre-stop layer\nthe route was about to extend — are vacuous and unused.  The endpoint already runs\nthe deterministic-partition square-increment estimate\n\\(\\mathbb E[M_t^2]=\\sum_i \\mathbb E[(M_{t_{i+1}}-M_{t_i})^2]\\le \\mathbb E[\\max_i|\\Delta M|\\cdot V]\\to 0\\)\n(increment orthogonality from\nLemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.integral_increment_mul_increment_eq_zero}) and the terminal\ncore Lemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_bounded_continuous_finiteVariation_core},\nyielding \\(M_t=0\\) almost surely.\n\\end{proof}"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6678,"cache_read_input_tokens":146430,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6678},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c68c3a1-ce96-4aaa-9a82-bf5af151dc85","uuid":"ad9dd123-c84d-499a-b464-1d0646201ca2","request_id":"req_011CbshHfHu1bABUZUneZqaB"}
